 (5+ drinks/day) as metabolic risk factor"], "excluded_candidates": ["Subacute Gynecological Emergency (e.g. ruptured ovarian cyst): Pain progression contradicted by sudden acute exacerbation", "Chronic Alcohol-Related Liver Disease: Acute hemodynamic instability and RUQ pain suggest acute rather than chronic pathology", "Endocrine Emergency (e.g. adrenal insufficiency): No specific evidence of endocrine dysfunction despite constitutional findings", "Acute Pancreatitis of Alcohol-Induced or Biliary Origin: Requires further imaging/labs to confirm vs. infectious causes"], "alarm_features": ["Hemodynamic instability (hypotension + tachycardia)", "Uncharacterised shock mechanism", "Recent sexually transmitted infection history", "Significant alcohol use as metabolic risk factor"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic Emergency with Hemodynamic Instability", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.5813953488372094, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Lack of biliary obstruction's typical biliary tract signs (dark urine, clay stools) moderately argues against common bile duct obstruction (B3) as the primary …"], "evidence_against": ["Absence of Budd-Chiari syndrome's hallmark features (jaundice, ascites) provides strong evidence against hepatic vein thrombosis (B2) as the cause of hemodynam…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Emergency", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2, "posterior": 0.23255813953488375, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Lack of adnexal signs and no missed period weakly argues against ectopic pregnancy (B1) as the primary gynecologic cause of shock and RUQ pain."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of biliary obstruction's typical biliary tract signs (dark urine, clay stools) moderately argues against common bile duct obstruction (B3) as the primary …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Biliary Obstruction with Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15, "posterior": 0.1744186046511628, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Acute RUQ pain onset during physical activity weakly supports biliary obstruction (B3) over chronic disease, as gallstone migration can cause sudden colicky pa…"], "evidence_against": ["Lack of biliary obstruction's typical biliary tract signs (dark urine, clay stools) moderately argues against common bile duct obstruction (B3) as the primary …"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.55, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Pancreatic Emergency", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.012, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of adnexal signs and no missed period weakly argues against ectopic pregnancy (B1) as the primary gynecologic cause of shock and RUQ pain."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25839793281653756, "posterior": 0.25839793281653756, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Ovarian Torsion with Ischemia", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.19379844961240314, "posterior": 0.19379844961240314, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Ruptured Corpus Luteum Hemorrhage", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12919896640826878, "posterior": 0.12919896640826878, "danger": 0.65, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the recent gonococcal urethritis history with unprotected sexual activity and pending β-hCG test provide stronger evidence for a gynecologic emergency than for other causes of shock and RUQ pain?", "summary": "The recent sexually transmitted infection history and unprotected sex moderately support gynecologic causes of shock and RUQ pain, particularly ectopic implantation (Option A). Pending β-hCG test results will clarify this."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the heavy alcohol consumption and acute hemodynamic collapse during exercise provide stronger evidence for Budd-Chiari syndrome than for other hepatic causes?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol abuse and sudden hemodynamic collapse during exercise moderately support Budd-Chiari syndrome (B2) as a hepatic cause, though acute presentations are rare in this condition."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the sudden RUQ pain exacerbation during exercise suggest gallstone-induced biliary obstruction rather than chronic biliary disease?", "summary": "Acute RUQ pain onset during physical activity weakly supports biliary obstruction (B3) over chronic disease, as gallstone migration can cause sudden colicky pain."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of adnexal tenderness or missed period in the vignette argue against ectopic pregnancy as the primary gynecologic cause of shock and RUQ pain?", "summary": "Lack of adnexal signs and no missed period weakly argues against ectopic pregnancy (B1) as the primary gynecologic cause of shock and RUQ pain."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of jaundice, ascites, or splanchnic vein thrombosis in the vignette contradict the hepatic vein thrombosis mechanism required for Budd-Chiari syndrome?", "summary": "Absence of Budd-Chiari syndrome's hallmark features (jaundice, ascites) provides strong evidence against hepatic vein thrombosis (B2) as the cause of hemodynamic instability."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of jaundice, dark urine, or clay-colored stools in the vignette inconsistent with common bile duct obstruction as the primary pathology?", "summary": "Lack of biliary obstruction's typical biliary tract signs (dark urine, clay stools) moderately argues against common bile duct obstruction (B3) as the primary pathology."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.534], ["B2", 0.308], ["B1", 0.153]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.581], ["B1.1", 0.258], ["B2", 0.233]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hemodynamic instability (BP 80/40 mmHg, HR 110 bpm) meets universal shock criteria requiring immediate resuscitation before diagnostic clarification", "required_actions": ["Aggressive IV fluid administration", "Monitor for end-organ dysfunction", "Consider septic shock workup", "Prepare for potential blood transfusion"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B1.1: Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage) has posterior 0.258 < 0.75; dangerous alternative B1.2 (posterior 0.194) remains unresolved. Emergency management (IV fluids) is ongoing, but diagnostic refinement is still needed to exclude life-threatening causes."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.45, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.25839793281653756, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Pancreatic calcifications', 'Acute pancreatitis', 'Abnormality of the intrahepatic bile duct'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B3", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.534}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage", "posterior": 0.258, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B1.2", "B3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Complications vs Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, fever, jaundice\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Complications vs Ruptured Corpus Luteum Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, fever, jaundice\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, cardiovascular collapse, dizziness, fainting, hypotension\n\nEctopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage vs Ruptured Corpus Luteum Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: cardiovascular collapse, dizziness, hypotension, stabbing pain in abdomen, syncope\n\nRelated to Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage: abdominal ectopic pregnancy, female reproductive system disease, ovarian ectopic pregnancy\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → arthralgia → Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemorrhage"}
